.TH smi-new-tag "1" "June 2025" "SMI Toolset 0.92.0" "SMI Tag Command"
.SH NAME
smi-new-tag \- create a versioned Git tag and update the 'latest' tag
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-new-tag
[\fIversion\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \fBsmi-new-tag\fR command automates the creation of a versioned Git tag and force-updates the
\fBlatest\fR tag to point to it. This is useful for maintaining clear version history
and always having a pointer to the latest stable release.
.PP
When run, it performs the following:
.IP \[bu] 2
Takes the tags of the remote as they are, with
.B git fetch --force --prune --prune-tags --tags origin
, so a local tag that was moved or recreated in the meantime is overwritten and one that is gone
from the remote is dropped.
.IP \[bu] 2
Checks if the given version tag already exists, the remote ones included after that fetch.
.IP \[bu] 2
Creates an annotated tag with the version string as both name and message.
.IP \[bu] 2
Forces the \fBlatest\fR tag to point to the newly created version.
.IP \[bu] 2
Pushes those two tags, and only those two, to the remote repository.
.PP
The command fails if the version tag already exists, or if the version argument is missing.
.PP
Only the version tag and
.B latest
are pushed on purpose.
.B git push --tags
pushes every local tag, and on a build machine, which keeps the tags of every earlier build, one
stale tag that differs from the remote one is refused by the remote
.RB ( "already exists" )
and fails the whole push, and with it the build, after the new tag has already arrived. A build
that is repeated on a version that is already tagged therefore fails on the check, not halfway
through the push.
.SH OPTIONS
.PP
This script takes a single required positional argument:
.TP
\fIversion\fR
The new semantic version number to tag (e.g. \fB0.92.0\fR).
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Tag version \fB0.92.0\fR and update \fBlatest\fR tag:
.PP
.B smi-new-tag 0.92.0
.SH AUTHOR
.PP
Command written by Imre Tabur.
